Provenance: this work is a modern rewrite of The Unarchiver. Its legacy-format
support is provided by the newtua-formats crate family, whose decoders are
ported from XADMaster (© Dag Ågren and contributors, GNU LGPL). Because the
engine links those LGPL components, newtua-core is itself distributed under the
LGPL.

Vendored third-party code. The read half of the `cab` crate
(https://github.com/mdsteele/rust-cab, © Matthew D. Steele, MIT) is carried
inside this crate at src/vendor/cab/ rather than depended on, because the
one-pass folder reading the engine needs exists upstream only on an unreleased
branch, and a crate published to crates.io may not carry a git dependency. The
MIT licence text travels with it (src/vendor/cab/LICENSE-MIT), and
src/vendor/cab/VENDORED.md records the release it came from and every change.

Within that module, src/vendor/cab/quantum.rs is a port of XADMaster's
XADQuantumHandle (© MacPaw Inc., GNU LGPL v2.1 or later) — the same XADMaster
lineage described above, and one more reason this engine is distributed under
the LGPL. The Quantum method itself was created by David Stafford and adapted
by Microsoft; the public description of it is Matthew Russotto's.

The read half of the `rars` crate (https://github.com/bitplane/rars, © Gareth
Davidson) is carried at src/vendor/rars/ for a different reason: that release is
published and installable, but the engine keeps changes of its own to it and
does not follow its weekly releases. This is what decodes RAR today, from
RAR 1.3 through RAR 7, in pure Rust. Note a discrepancy that is upstream's own — release 0.4.8 declares "MIT OR Apache-2.0"
in Cargo.toml while the only license file in that repository is COPYING (WTFPL
with an added "don't blame me" clause), and no MIT or Apache text ships with it.
Both readings permit this use; the text that actually travelled with the code is
beside it at src/vendor/rars/COPYING, and src/vendor/rars/VENDORED.md records
the release, everything removed, and every change of ours.

The engine also depends on three forced forks of third-party crates —
newtua-apfs, newtua-hfsplus and newtua-cdfs — which retain the
licenses of their respective upstream projects. Each fork's README explains why it exists
and states the license it carries.

RAR support and the UnRAR license. There is no longer anything here to which
that license applies. RAR archives used to be read through libunrar, by way of
a fourth forced fork (newtua-unrar); its sources were fetched at build time and
carried a license of their own (© Alexander Roshal) permitting use in any
software that handles RAR archives while forbidding the building of a
RAR-compatible archiver. Since the vendored `rars` reader was wired to the RAR
handler, that dependency is gone from the build entirely: RAR is decoded in
pure Rust, and nothing in this crate is governed by the UnRAR license.

